## 4. The silent-_start attack

  x86_emit_sys.oo:36-67 is `x86_emit_linux64(msgs)`. It always emits
  the `main:` and `_start:` labels (lines 51-52) and the `mov $60, %rax;
  xor %rdi, %rdi; syscall` (lines 65-67). If `msgs` is empty, the
  `while i < n` rodata loop (lines 40-46) and write loop (lines 53-64)
  are no-ops, but the ELF still has `_start` and exits 0. The .s body
  is real AT&T that as+ld would link into a no-op binary.

  This is a real attack surface. A fixture with `pub fn main() { /* no
  println */ }` would hit the empty-msgs path, not the !saw_main path.

  Lock:
    - x86_emit_walk.oo:209-212 fires before x86_emit_linux64:
        if list_len(msgs) < 1 {
            println("ERR\tx86_emit\tresidual: need println(\"...\")
                     (not emit-c)");
            process_exit(1);
        }
    - `msgs` is empty, exit 1. The empty-_start ELF is not written.

  Note: this is the same ERR-on-empty-msgs gate the wasm printer uses
  for the if-hostile case. PV1's attack fails: the gate holds.

  Status: [x]. Silent _start is blocked by x86_emit_walk.oo:209-212.

## 5. The hostile other — leftover-plus-print

  x86_phalanx.oot:13-15 says leftover-plus-print ERRs even when
  another print was harvested (hostile other; leftover_ident). This
  is not PV1's lock. PV1 only owns the no-main shape. The
  leftover-plus-print and leftover-ident locks are owned by the
  harvest lane (DE1) and validated by other smokes. PV1 cites them
  only to mark scope.

  See: scripts/x86_hostile_leftover_smoke.oo and
  scripts/x86_leftover_ident_smoke.oo (named by ROADMAP.oot:72 and
  SHIPPED.oot:34).

